 ## Overview
-This method manages 1D transfer functions for volume rendering by mapping scalar density values (typically in the range [0, 255]) to RGBA colors and alpha values using piecewise cubic spline interpolation [S1, S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself by maintaining two parallel sets of control points (one for color, one for alpha) and caching the resulting interpolation in a fixed-size 256-entry indexed array (`indexedTransferFunction`) for $O(1)$ integer lookup, while simultaneously supporting smooth float-based evaluation via cubic splines [S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Cubic Spline Transfer Function](1811-cubic-spline-transfer-function.md) by adding explicit management of alpha/color separation and an indexed cache for performance [S2, unofficial].
+This method manages 1D transfer functions for volume rendering by mapping scalar density values (typically in the range [0, 255]) to RGBA colors and alpha values using piecewise cubic spline interpolation [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself by maintaining two parallel sets of control points (one for color, one for alpha) and caching the resulting interpolation in a fixed-size 256-entry indexed array (`indexedTransferFunction`) for $O(1)$ integer lookup, while simultaneously supporting smooth float-based evaluation via cubic splines [S2, S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Cubic Spline Transfer Function](1811-cubic-spline-transfer-function.md) by adding explicit management of alpha/color separation and an indexed cache for performance [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of control points for color (RGB vector + isoValue) and alpha (float + isoValue). The scalar domain is discretized into 256 integer levels (0–255).
-*   **Output:** A 4-component vector (RGBA) for any given scalar input, either via integer index (cached) or float value (interpolated).
+*   **Input:** A set of control points for color (RGB vector + isoValue) and alpha (float + isoValue). The scalar domain is discretized into 256 integer levels (0–255) [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A 4-component vector (RGBA) for any given scalar input, either via integer index (cached) or float value (interpolated) [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
